Yes, it is possible. Here is the construction and proof.

## Construction

Define $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ by

$$
f(x)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mathbf{1}_{[n,\;n+2^{-n}]}(x),
$$

i.e. $f(x)=1$ if $x\in[n,\,n+2^{-n}]$ for some positive integer $n$, and $f(x)=0$ otherwise.

## The intervals are disjoint

For $n\ge 1$ we have $2^{-n}\le \tfrac12$, so each interval $[n,n+2^{-n}]$ is contained in $[n,n+\tfrac12]$. Since consecutive integers are spaced by $1>\tfrac12$, the intervals $\{[n,n+2^{-n}]\}_{n\ge1}$ are pairwise disjoint. Hence $f$ is well defined (at most one term in the sum is nonzero) and $f$ takes only the values $0$ and $1$.

## $f$ is integrable over $\mathbb{R}$ (both Lebesgue and improper Riemann)

On any bounded interval $[a,b]$, only finitely many of the intervals $[n,n+2^{-n}]$ intersect $[a,b]$, so $f$ restricted to $[a,b]$ is a step function with finitely many jump discontinuities. In particular $f$ is Riemann integrable on every $[a,b]$.

The (Lebesgue, equivalently improper Riemann) integral over $\mathbb{R}$ is

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)\,dx
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl|(n+2^{-n})-n\bigr|
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}2^{-n}
= 1<\infty.
$$

The interchange of sum and integral is justified because the intervals are disjoint (the summands have disjoint supports), so this is a direct computation of the measure of $\bigcup_n[n,n+2^{-n}]$. Thus $f\in L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$ and the improper Riemann integral $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f$ converges.

## $f(x)\not\to 0$ as $|x|\to\infty$

For every positive integer $n$,

$$
f(n)=1,
$$

since $n\in[n,n+2^{-n}]$. Hence there exists a sequence $x_n=n\to+\infty$ with $f(x_n)=1$ for all $n$. Therefore $f(x)$ does **not** tend to $0$ as $x\to+\infty$ (and hence not as $|x|\to\infty$).

## Conclusion

The function $f$ is integrable over the entire real line (with total integral $1$), yet $f(n)=1$ for every $n\in\mathbb{N}$, so its values do not approach zero as $|x|\to\infty$.

**Remark.** This does not contradict the standard theorem that *a uniformly continuous* integrable function must vanish at infinity. The obstruction is exactly the lack of regularity: our $f$ has jumps of size $1$ at every integer, so it is not uniformly continuous. The counterexample shows that some regularity hypothesis (such as uniform continuity, monotonicity, or continuity with bounded variation) is essential for the "integrable $\Rightarrow$ vanishes at infinity" conclusion.

